I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, refine them jointly, and verify that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and GSAS-II project all come from the same final model. I’ll keep phase assignments conservative where the pattern does not uniquely support a structure.

The chemistry strongly suggests cobalt tungstate formation, but I’m treating that only as a candidate: the decisive checks will be indexed peak positions, whole-pattern residuals, and whether precursor/secondary-phase peaks remain after joint refinement. I’m now extracting the observed peak set and querying the local structure database for Co–W–O candidates.

Peak positions match two precursor phases with a coherent reflection-geometry displacement: monoclinic γ-WO₃ (P2₁/n) and spinel Co₃O₄ (Fd-3m). The diagnostic WO₃ triplet and the Co₃O₄ 311/333/440 series are all present; the strongest expected CoWO₄-only lines are not. I’m moving to a joint Rietveld refinement, including the Cu Kα doublet and a specimen-displacement correction before refining lattice constants and phase scales.

The newer neutron-derived WO₃ model materially improves the fit (Rwp 3.39% versus 4.37%) and removes the scale singularity; the refined crystalline fractions are currently about 71.6 wt% WO₃ and 28.4 wt% Co₃O₄. I still see structured residuals at the strongest WO₃ clusters, so I’m testing whether they reflect legitimately refinable heavy-atom coordinates or texture/profile effects before freezing the final model.

The phase fractions are stable to background order (about 71.5/28.5 wt% across reasonable choices), while an overly flexible background begins to absorb broad peak tails. I’m keeping a moderate background model and now checking the last major systematic risk for quantitative analysis—preferred orientation—before final export.

The preferred-orientation tests refined essentially to unity, so texture is not driving the phase fractions. I’m running one final polymorph challenge against triclinic WO₃; it is slower because the lower symmetry greatly expands the reflection/parameter set, but it is the right check before calling the monoclinic assignment definitive.

The triclinic challenge is rejected: it fits worse despite extra freedom, and its refined angles move back toward 90°, supporting monoclinic γ-WO₃. I’ve also extended the final refinement to every measured point; the final model converges cleanly at 72.52 wt% WO₃ and 27.48 wt% Co₃O₄, with Rwp 3.361%. I’m exporting all deliverables from that exact project now.

The refinement and required deliverables are in place. I’m doing a final consistency check now—reloading the GSAS-II project, validating both CIFs, and confirming that the reported fractions and profile arrays exactly match the saved project.

The CIFs parse cleanly and reconstruct the expected full-cell compositions (WO₃ and Co₃O₄) with space groups 14 and 227. I’m checking the saved project itself with the GSAS-II runtime next; this is the last substantive verification.

